\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 2. Top 3 Risks to Monitor\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Strategic opacity read as untrustworthiness.
\f0\b0  Moderate Machiavellianism plus high humility can combine into a leader who thinks carefully but explains too little. Silence gets filled in by others' imaginations, usually with a worse story than the truth.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ethical erosion under acute pressure.
\f0\b0  Your ethical-leadership score is a real strength, but it's a muscle, not a fixed trait \'97 it's most likely to weaken in a specific high-stakes moment (a deadline, a political fight, protecting a project or a person you're loyal to), not gradually over time. The risk is a single bad exception, not a slow slide.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Under-advocacy that costs you and your team.
\f0\b0  High humility can mean you don't claim credit, don't push back on a bad decision from above, or let your empathy-driven fairness impulse lead you to under-negotiate for your own team's resources.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
