\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 High Ethical Leadership (79).
\f0\b0  This is your governing strength \'97 it's the trait most correlated with sustainable trust and long-term team loyalty. It directly offsets the Machiavellian risk: an explicit personal ethical framework, stated out loud to your team, converts "strategic" from a suspicious word into a legible one, because people can see the principle behind the strategy.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 High Empathy (64).
\f0\b0  This is what keeps your strategic thinking oriented toward people rather than around them. It offsets the sadism/psychopathy risk almost entirely (both are already low) \'97 but more importantly, empathy is the trait that lets you 
\f2\i notice
\f0\i0  when your strategic instincts are landing badly on someone, in time to adjust.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 High Humility (82).
\f0\b0  This is what makes you coachable, and it's rare in people who also score high on ethical leadership \'97 the combination is what research on this profile would call a genuinely low-risk, high-trust leadership style. The way it offsets the Machiavellian streak: humble leaders ask "how did that land?" instead of assuming their strategic read was correct, which catches manipulation-adjacent behavior before it does damage.\
